WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.120
Whatever the reason, a character's departing&nbsp;
expression says a lot about who they were and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.080
stories in which they played their final roles.&nbsp;
Whether disturbing, beautiful, or something else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.880
altogether, these dying smiles cemented the&nbsp;
legacies of the characters who offered them.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.400
Inside Out famously left audiences of&nbsp;
all ages in tears. The story centers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.080
around a young girl named Riley, who's&nbsp;
struggling to adjust after a big move.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.120
She's in the midst of new and increasingly&nbsp;
complicated feelings that come with getting older,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.240
and inside her head, the personifications&nbsp;
of Joy and Sadness are on a quest to restore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.920
some semblance of order. They eventually meet&nbsp;
Bing Bong, Riley's childhood imaginary friend.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:41.280
"Excuse me?"

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.200
"Uh, I was looking for…&nbsp;
diversion! So long sucker!"

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.600
As the trio tries to heal the emotional&nbsp;
wounds that threaten to overwhelm Riley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.240
Joy and Bing Bong end up in the Memory Dump, a&nbsp;
canyon of forgotten memories that are eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:03.360
erased forever. Desperate, the two try to escape&nbsp;
on Bing Bong's wagon, which propels itself with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.960
rainbows and is fueled by song and belief. Joy&nbsp;
and Bing Bong take care of the singing, but Riley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.680
has lost touch with her faith and imagination,&nbsp;
and the wagon can't make it to the top. Bing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.480
Bong realizes that without his weight, the wagon&nbsp;
will go the distance, and he sacrifices himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.040
This means that Riley will forget about him,&nbsp;
but as Joy flies to freedom, he smiles because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.000
he knows Riley will grow up with the emotional&nbsp;
health to make so many more wonderful memories.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.480
"Take her to the moon for me, ok?"

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.320
Tim Burton's films are often dark, but they&nbsp;
all have his trademark whimsical humor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.120
The combination is often surreal, and&nbsp;
his 1989 take on Batman is no exception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.200
right down to the death of the Joker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.080
The fusion of gothic sensibilities with a certain&nbsp;
sense of wonder feels just a little demented,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.120
making Burton's filmmaking style a great medium&nbsp;
through which to portray this particular villain.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:00.800
Speaking of gothic, the Joker dies on the steps of&nbsp;
a cathedral after Batman tethers him to a gargoyle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.440
that later plummets to the ground. Before his&nbsp;
demise, he dances happily while waiting for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.560
getaway helicopter. Of course, he takes the moment&nbsp;
to gloat and laugh at Batman and Vicki Vale.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.800
The joke's on him, however - it's his own&nbsp;
helicopter that tugs him and the gargoyle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:20.160
free from the ledge, so he truly is responsible&nbsp;
for his own death. But even in his final moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.560
he remains psychotic and smiling, like he's&nbsp;
the only one who understands the punchline.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:30.160
The Princess Bride introduced audiences to a&nbsp;
conniving villain by the name of Vizzini , a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.520
genius-level thug who kidnaps Princess Buttercup&nbsp;
and draws the ire of the Dread Pirate Roberts — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.160
buccaneer who's secretly Buttercup's long-lost&nbsp;
lover, Westley. When Westley catches up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.720
the kidnapper, the two have an odd challenge&nbsp;
in mind to decide things once and for all.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.360
Westley and Vizzini engage in a battle of wits, in&nbsp;
which the latter must choose between two goblets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.360
knowing one is poisoned. Vizzini&nbsp;
has a trick up his sleeve, though.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.200
"What in the world can that be?"

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.880
"What, where? I don't see anything."

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.880
He switches the goblets when Westley's&nbsp;
back is turned, thinking he has tricked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.440
his enemy. In reality, Westley has prepared&nbsp;
for this moment by poisoning himself for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:13.040
so long that he's become immune to the toxin.&nbsp;
As it turns out, he's contaminated both cups.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.160
As smart as Vizzini thinks he&nbsp;
is, he never would've imagined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.960
that his enemy would poison himself. He dies&nbsp;
laughing, reveling in his perceived triumph,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:25.360
with no idea that it was the body, not&nbsp;
the mind, that would decide this battle.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:26.560
"Inconceivable!"

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.240
Titanic introduced us to the&nbsp;
king of the world himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.200
Jack Dawson. He's a working-class rogue who falls&nbsp;
for the aristocratic Rose, and by the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:40.240
film, he's totally stolen her heart. However,&nbsp;
when the titular ship crashes into that iceberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.720
it sends our lovestruck heroes plummeting&nbsp;
into the ocean, leading to a frosty finale.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.960
As Rose floats above the water on a wooden&nbsp;
door, Jack is half-submerged at her side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:53.920
There's apparently nothing else for him to grab&nbsp;
onto, and Jack pleads with Rose to never give up.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:04:01.360
"You're gonna get out of here, you're gonna&nbsp;
go on, and you're gonna make lots of babies."

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.840
If she can just stay afloat, help will&nbsp;
come, leading to happiness even after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.920
he's gone. Jack then falls unconscious&nbsp;
due to hypothermia and never wakes up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:15.040
but one of the last things he does before dying is&nbsp;
smile at the woman he loves. Some say you see your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.600
life flash before your eyes in your last moments,&nbsp;
but it seems like Jack saw Rose's instead.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.880
Smiles that precede sacrifice often come&nbsp;
from recognizing a bigger cause than one's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:29.920
own survival. Steve Trevor's final sacrifice&nbsp;
saves the love of his life and all of humanity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:35.440
so that's a big win for him. His love is the&nbsp;
titular hero of 2017's Wonder Woman, who tasks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:40.400
herself with confronting Ares . The god of war&nbsp;
threatens to destroy humanity with World War I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.040
and this includes manipulating an evil scientist&nbsp;
who's engineered a new form of mustard gas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.480
In his final sacrifice, Steve flies a plane&nbsp;
containing this poison to a safe altitude before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:54.240
it's scheduled to detonate, destroying&nbsp;
the poison and himself in the process.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:58.880
He offers up his own life because he loves&nbsp;
her, but he also demonstrates a love for all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.440
of humanity. Steve became concrete proof&nbsp;
of what she'd doubted — that humans have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:07.840
goodness and love in them and are therefore&nbsp;
worth saving. Steve smiled in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.880
because he knew he had given&nbsp;
love a second chance to prevail.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:16.960
"It has to be me, it has to be me. I&nbsp;
can save today. You can save the world."

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.880
Despite giving his film the title of Kill Bill:&nbsp;
Vol. 2, writer-director Quentin Tarantino kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:26.880
audiences guessing as to whether Beatrix&nbsp;
"The Bride" Kiddo would take her former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:31.360
lover and longtime enemy to meet his maker&nbsp;
for the massacre he committed at her wedding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.280
When she finally vanquishes him, he&nbsp;
smiles after she delivers the final blow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.800
and he has many reasons. He's made peace with&nbsp;
her. He has a daughter. He's mourned Beatrix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:46.880
then tried to kill her, and is now dying at her&nbsp;
hands. The ending is as poetic as each deserves.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.040
Bill isn't the only one who greets his death&nbsp;
with a degree of good humor. Beatrix does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.800
as well, even if it's artificial. Bill&nbsp;
didn't want her daughter to see her in tears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.800 --> 00:06:00.400
so when she mourns him the next day her tears are&nbsp;
mixed with uncontrollable laughter. Bill knew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:04.080
or perhaps hoped, that she would need&nbsp;
to cry because she still loved him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.280
and perhaps this is the ultimate&nbsp;
reason for his dying smile.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:12.240
It makes a lot of sense that The Green&nbsp;
Mile's John Coffey — a savior figure who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.240
shares the initials and miraculous healing&nbsp;
abilities of Jesus Christ — would have an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:20.240
uncommon take on death. His expression is&nbsp;
strained as he waits, wrongly convicted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:24.160
in the electric chair to be executed.&nbsp;
But it definitely looks like a smile.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.280
The most striking aspect of John's death was that&nbsp;
he chose to die. The dark side to his supernatural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.160
healing abilities was the constant pain he felt&nbsp;
as an intimate witness to the cruelty of humanity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:36.720
John spent his time on Earth&nbsp;
lifting this burden from others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.320
but in choosing with his death to reveal the&nbsp;
cruelty of the world, he shared the burden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.120
with Paul Edgecomb . We can imagine that John&nbsp;
smiled with relief of a weight finally lifted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.440
and maybe even some hope that humanity might&nbsp;
learn to share its burdens and heal itself.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:53.600
People often find themselves smiling&nbsp;
when embarking on a new adventure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:58.480
and Albus Dumbledore described death as the&nbsp;
next great adventure. When his on-screen end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:03.360
came in Harry Potter and the Half-Blood Prince,&nbsp;
he was ready. His smile was that of a conspirator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:08.000
playing everyone like fiddles. He'd accepted&nbsp;
his death long ago, when he was irreversibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.720
infected by a cursed ring. He knew he had to&nbsp;
die, and if it happened by Severus Snape's hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:17.520
Voldemort would trust Snape, who could&nbsp;
then protect Harry from behind enemy lines.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:21.840
Dumbledore smiled because he knew Harry was&nbsp;
still on the road to fulfilling his destiny.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:26.240
Additionally, by asking Snape to do the&nbsp;
deed, both wizards spared Draco from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:30.480
the heinous act that would undoubtedly&nbsp;
propel him past the point of no return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:33.600
leaving the misguided boy with&nbsp;
hope for redemption. Most of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:37.440
Dumbledore knew his death would be his&nbsp;
greatest service to the wizarding world.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:42.880
The theory behind Obi-Wan Kenobi's pre-mortem&nbsp;
smile, the one just before Darth Vader's killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:47.680
blow in Star Wars Episode IV: A New Hope, has&nbsp;
developed over the course of future revelations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:53.040
in the franchise. Eventually, we realize that&nbsp;
Obi-Wan knew the true parentage of Luke and Leia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:57.840
even if the nature of the relationship&nbsp;
was decided after the 1977 film premiered.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:01.600
"Help me Obi Wan Kenobi, you're my only hope."

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:02.160
"What's this?"

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:06.080
"What is what? He asked you&nbsp;
a question, what is that?"

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.720
Originally, it seemed like he could have&nbsp;
smiled because he was about to commune with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:15.120
the Force or because he knew Luke would escape,&nbsp;
along with the hope of defeating the Empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:18.000
But considering the information&nbsp;
acquired from subsequent films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:22.240
it could be imagined that his smile had&nbsp;
to do with Luke and Leia being reunited.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:25.040
More than just a heartwarming&nbsp;
moment for the Skywalker family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:29.280
Luke and Leia's reunion represented the&nbsp;
idea that neither time nor distance nor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:34.160
any other seemingly indomitable force, not&nbsp;
even THE Force, was more powerful than love.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:41.360
It feels so good to finally get a joke - just&nbsp;
ask Mr. Dawes Sr. from the original Mary Poppins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:45.360
The elder Dawes is a classic greedy&nbsp;
boss, and just before he dies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.640
he chides employee George Banks for using&nbsp;
the word supercalifragilisticexpialidocious

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.000
George asks him if he'd like to hear&nbsp;
a joke, which goes, "There were these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:58.160
two wonderful young people, Jane and&nbsp;
Michael. And they meet one day on the street,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:01.920
and Jane says to Michael, 'I know a&nbsp;
man with a wooden leg named Smith.'

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:07.840
"And Michael says 'Really? What's&nbsp;
the name of his other leg?'"

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:12.320
Dawes at first thinks that George has&nbsp;
gone mad, not yet understanding the joke.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:16.320
He repeats the punchline over and over&nbsp;
to himself until he finally gets it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:19.920
and then he begins laughing uncontrollably&nbsp;
before floating into the air.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:23.360
He eventually dies of laughter, which&nbsp;
George regrets upon finding out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:27.760
but the greedy old man's son assures George&nbsp;
that he'd never seen his father happier.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.680
The ending of Donnie Darko&nbsp;
is notoriously confusing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:33.920
The title character in this film dies laughing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.800
finally finding peace and relief in a cosmic&nbsp;
role that has eluded him throughout his life.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:43.840
This role befalls him by chance. An unspecified&nbsp;
disruption creates a tangent universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:49.120
which is a duplicate of the primary universe in&nbsp;
every way except for one anomaly — a jet engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:53.920
which is duplicated twice. With no logical&nbsp;
place in the tangent universe, the extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:58.720
copy happens to fall on Donnie's house. Donnie,&nbsp;
likely because he happened to be at the center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:04.000
of the tangent universe's genesis, is chosen to&nbsp;
guide the anomaly out of that universe to safely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:09.760
collapse the timeline. The only way to do so is to&nbsp;
send it through a portal to the primary universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:17.840
where it falls onto Donnie's house,&nbsp;
as well, this time with him inside.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:18.115
"Why are you wearing that stupid man suit?"

00:10:18.115 --> 00:10:21.520
Donnie likely finds comfort in the fact that&nbsp;
he was right about this tangent universe and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:27.280
not crazy, at least not in the way that everyone&nbsp;
told him he was. He also probably smiled shortly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:31.840
before death because, by stabilizing the&nbsp;
timelines, he saved the people he loved.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:37.280
Independence Day was a huge hit with audiences&nbsp;
in 1996, partly because of a colorful ensemble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:42.160
of characters that included retired pilot&nbsp;
Russell Casse, an alien abductee who volunteers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:48.080
to confront an alien warship attacking Area&nbsp;
51. Eventually, Russell is the only pilot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:52.480
with a missile available to compromise the&nbsp;
warship's weapons port, but it malfunctions.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:56.080
Russell recognizes that he can complete&nbsp;
his mission by piloting his craft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:01.360
directly into its target, which will kill him&nbsp;
in the process. He tells his compatriots to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:06.320
give his love to his family, then crashes into&nbsp;
the warship with a grin and a gleeful shout.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:14.720
"Hello boys. I'm baaaack!"

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:18.960
In the end, the most important thing for Russell&nbsp;
was the safety of his family, as well as a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:23.760
revenge against the aliens who abducted him. His&nbsp;
final moments were filled with the kind of purpose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:27.840
that creates a legend — and enough adrenaline&nbsp;
to follow through on that purpose with a smile.

